@page "/circuit"

<PageTitle>Circuit · BevTools</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <p class="bb-overline">Scenario · Blazor Server</p>
    <h1>Circuit</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Circuit problems: blocking the circuit's single thread, and killing the circuit with an unhandled exception.
        BevTools → Circuit and the Timeline show the circuit's events, the time each inbound message took, and the
        reconnect state reported by the browser.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> The circuit</h3>
    @if (RendererInfo.Name != "Server")
    {
        <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
            This page is running on the <strong>@RendererInfo.Name</strong> renderer. Circuit scenarios only apply to
            Interactive Server; under Interactive Auto the app switches to WebAssembly once the runtime is cached. Open
            the site in a fresh private window (or clear site data) to get a Server circuit, then come back here.
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="bb-card-desc">
            Each user of a Blazor Server app has one circuit, and it processes one message at a time: while a handler
            blocks, nothing else for that user is handled. The last button ends the circuit for good: Blazor shows its
            error bar rather than the reconnect dialog, because a circuit that failed is not coming back.
        </p>
    }

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span><RendererStatus /></div>

        <div class="bb-toolbar">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="Block">Block the circuit for 800 ms (Thread.Sleep in a handler)</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="BlockRepeatedly">Block 5× in a row</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn demo-btn-danger" @onclick="Kill">Unhandled exception in a background task (terminates the circuit)</button>
        </div>
        <p class="bb-muted">Blocked: @_blocked times. Renderer: @RendererInfo.Name, interactive: @RendererInfo.IsInteractive.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Blocking: slow inbound messages</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Block the circuit. The <strong>Timeline</strong> shows a <em>Slow inbound message</em>, the
        <strong>Circuit</strong> panel the maximum inbound processing time, and <strong>Diagnostics</strong> the slow
        event handler. The fix: never block in a handler - await the work instead.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Killing: the circuit's last moments</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Each circuit has a BevTools session of its own, and the panel renders over that circuit, so when the circuit
        dies the panel stops where it was. Open the <strong>companion window</strong> (⧉ in the panel header) first,
        then kill the circuit: the companion reads the page itself, so <strong>Network → WebSockets</strong> shows the
        circuit's last message, a <code>JS.Error</code>, and its socket closing, and the status line says the runtime
        is disconnected. Reload for a new circuit and a new session.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <a href="problems#problem-9" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 9 →</a>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private int _blocked;

    private void Block()
    {
        // Intentional problem: synchronous blocking inside the circuit. Nothing else for this user is processed meanwhile.
        Thread.Sleep(800);
        _blocked++;
    }

    private async Task BlockRepeatedly()
    {
        for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        {
            Thread.Sleep(300);
            _blocked++;
            StateHasChanged();
            await Task.Delay(50);
        }
    }

    private void Kill()
    {
        // Background work reports its failure to the renderer with DispatchExceptionAsync. With no ErrorBoundary around
        // the page that is fatal for the circuit; BevTools captures the logged error first. (A throw inside InvokeAsync
        // would only fault the discarded task and nothing would happen.)
        _ = Task.Run(async () =>
        {
            await Task.Delay(100);
            await DispatchExceptionAsync(new InvalidOperationException("Simulated unhandled exception dispatched to the circuit."));
        });
    }
}
